            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX ? New_Ret >= 0 : New_Ret == -1);

        }
    }
}

```

#### Parameterized unit tests
Unit tests are also great for parametrizing so we can run the same unit test for different parameter scenarios. This ensures that the function behaves as expected in different conditions. Let's parameterize the unit tests using the PexSettings attribute.

Let's parameterize our unit test using different arrayLists and values of x:

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [PexClass(MaxExceptions = 100, MaxStates = 100)]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexInlineData(new int[] {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, 1),
                                        PexInlineData(new int[] {10,20,30,40,50}, 3),
                                        PexInlineData(new int[] {100,200,300,400,500}, 5)]
                                       int[] arrList, int x)
        {
            //..Unit test code here
        }
    }
}
```
This parameterized unit test runs the same unit test multiple times with different parameters. Note that we are now passing an array of integers for the test parameter to test the IndexOf method for various ArrayList input scenarios.

Remember that unit testing is an agile practice that developers can apply regardless of what the final method or module might be. It is a best practice to regularly run unit tests to ensure the reliability of your code.


# Wrapping Up
In this blog, we explored the importance of unit tests, how they aid in code reliability, how to write a unit test for the ArrayList.IndexOf method, how to run unit tests using Visual Studio, and how to parameterize unit tests for various scenarios. Writing effective unit tests can save hours of debugging and maintainable code.

I hope this information helped and I am available if you have more questions. Please feel free to contact me.

Happy coding. Enjoy unit testing. ###

